---
import StarlightPage from "@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro";
import { DOCS_ENTRY, PACKAGE, REPO } from "../lib/agent-metadata";

// Two questions decide whether anyone adopts a small package, and neither was answered
// anywhere on this site: who stands behind it, and what it deliberately does not do. The
// landing page argues for shorn and the docs assume you have already chosen it, so a
// reader weighing it up had to infer both from the source.
const root = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const href = (to: string) => (to.startsWith("http") ? to : `${root}${to}`);
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: "About shorn",
    description:
      "What shorn does, where it does not fit, who maintains it, and where its source and measurements live.",
    tableOfContents: false,
  }}
>
  <p>
    shorn is a small TypeScript library that turns a validation schema you already have
    into a compact binary format. It works with anything that implements <a
      href="https://standardschema.dev">Standard Schema</a
    >, which today means Zod, Valibot, and ArkType. It is published on npm as <code
      >{PACKAGE}</code
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>The problem it solves</h2>

  <p>
    JSON writes every field name into every payload and then parses that text back on the
    way in. Binary formats fix both, but the established ones ask for something in return:
    Protobuf wants a <code>.proto</code> file and a code generation step, Avro wants its
    own schema, SchemaPack wants the shape repeated through its builder. Now the same data
    shape exists twice, in two languages, and the two can drift apart.
  </p>

  <p>
    shorn removes the second copy. Your validator already knows every field, its type, and
    its order, so shorn reads the wire layout straight from it. You pass the same schema to
    <code>encode</code> and <code>decode</code>, and the field names and type tags leave
    the bytes because both ends can derive them. There is nothing to define twice, nothing
    to generate, and nothing to keep in step.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it does not fit</h2>

  <p>
    shorn is for payloads whose two ends are both TypeScript and can import the same
    schema module: caches, RPC, WebSocket frames, worker messages, and job queues. Outside
    that, pick something else, and the <a href={href("/comparisons/")}>comparisons page</a>
    says which:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      It is not a cross-language format. A reader written in Go or Python has no way to
      decode the bytes.
    </li>
    <li>
      Payloads are not self-describing. Without the schema they are just bytes, so reach
      for MessagePack or CBOR if a reader has to decode blind.
    </li>
    <li>
      It does not migrate a payload across a schema change. <code>fingerprinted()</code> can
      detect that the shape moved and reject the payload, but it will not upgrade it the way
      Protobuf or Avro would.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who maintains it</h2>

  <p>
    One person: Rahul Singh, on GitHub as <a href="https://github.com/ChiChuRita"
      >ChiChuRita</a
    >. There is no company behind shorn, no team, and no paid tier. Every change goes
    through the same public repository, and <a href={REPO + "/issues"}>GitHub issues</a> is
    the only support channel.
  </p>

  <h2>Licence and status</h2>

  <p>
    MIT licensed, and an experimental alpha. The version is still below 1.0, so neither the
    API nor the byte layout is frozen. Any change to the bytes a schema produces is treated
    as breaking however small the diff, and is called out in the release notes, so read
    those before upgrading if you have payloads in storage or in a queue. It is ESM only
    and needs Node 20 or newer, or any modern browser.
  </p>

  <h2>Source and measurements</h2>

  <p>
    The library, its tests, and its benchmarks are all in <a href={REPO}>one repository</a>.
    The benchmarks compare shorn against msgpackr, cbor-x, Avro, Protobuf, SchemaPack, and
    JSON, and every number published here comes from running them rather than from an
    estimate. The <a href={href("/performance/size/")}>performance pages</a> name the
    machine and the Node version behind each table and give the command that reproduces it.
    <a href={href(DOCS_ENTRY)}>The documentation</a> starts with what shorn is and how the
    encode and decode path works.
  </p>
</StarlightPage>
